1 Introduction
Large Language Models (LLMs) (
[46](#bib.bib1)
;
[11](#bib.bib10)
) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in various domains, including mathematical reasoning, question answering, and code generation. However, the knowledge encoded in these models is static, limiting their adaptability. As a result, LLMs are susceptible to producing hallucinated or outdated responses (
[16](#bib.bib2)
;
[34](#bib.bib3)
;
[1](#bib.bib30)
) when dealing with complex or real-time issues, compromising their reliability. Therefore, it is essential to equip LLMs with access to external knowledge to ensure more accurate and grounded responses.
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) (
[9](#bib.bib4)
;
[8](#bib.bib5)
) is a widely adopted approach to address this issue, broadening the model's capability boundaries by integrating external knowledge into the generation process. Early efforts (
[31](#bib.bib18)
;
[27](#bib.bib6)
) in this area concentrated on prompt-based strategies that guide LLMs through question decomposition, query rewriting and multi-turn retrieval. While effective, these approaches are constrained by the limitations inherent in prompt engineering. Recent advances (
[38](#bib.bib19)
;
[21](#bib.bib20)
;
[1](#bib.bib30)
;
[10](#bib.bib31)
;
[40](#bib.bib32)
) have increasingly emphasized the integration of reasoning capabilities within RAG. These approaches combine RAG with Chain of Thought (CoT) for step-by-step retrieval, or automatically generate intermediate retrieval chains that incorporate reasoning and employ Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) to enable learning of retrieval and reasoning. However, new insights (
[3](#bib.bib21)
) indicate that these methods may cause models to memorize solution paths, thus constraining their generalization to novel scenarios."]

