1. Introduction
There are two typical paradigms to improve RAG systems: the joint training approach v.s. training different components separately. The first paradigm involves joint training of LLMs and retrievers on knowledge-intensive tasks, enhancing retrieval capabilities of language models (
[Izacard et al. 2022](#bib.bib14)
) . For example,
[Guu et al. 2020](#bib.bib11)
did joint training of LLM and a retriever's semantic embedding, and their approach has shown promising results. However, it lacks interpretability because the communication between LLMs and retrievers relies on complex deep-learning gradient propagation and cross-attention between IR embedding models and LLMs. Furthermore, this training approach is very computationally expensive, and it's very hard or expensive to retrain the retriever's semantic embedding as LLMs change or learn. The second paradigm improves LLM and/or IR engines separately. Most prior work in this paradigm focuses on improving LLM (LLM-centric), either through prompting or fine-tuning LLM parameters (
[Ram et al. 2023](#bib.bib34)
;
[Lazaridou et al. 2022](#bib.bib20)
;
[Nakano et al. 2021](#bib.bib30)"]

